# Typed pipe-lambda parameters, added 2026-08-07.
#
# Regression spec for a parser gap found while a lane was writing lambda
# specs: `|x: i64| x + 1` (typed pipe-lambda param) was rejected with
# `Unexpected token: expected Pipe, found Colon`, while the untyped form
# `|x| x + 1` parsed fine. Confirmed on 2026-08-07 that BOTH
# `bin/simple run` (JIT, falls back to interpreter) and
# `SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpret bin/simple run` fail identically with the
# same parse error -- the parser is a shared frontend, so this was not
# engine-specific.
#
# Root cause: `parse_pipe_lambda_params`
# (src/compiler_rust/parser/src/expressions/postfix.rs) and
# `parse_remaining_lambda_params`
# (src/compiler_rust/parser/src/expressions/helpers.rs) pushed every
# `LambdaParam` with `ty: None` unconditionally and never checked for an
# optional `: Type` after the parameter name -- unlike `fn` parameter list
# parsing, which already supports `name: Type`. The `LambdaParam` AST node
# already had a `ty: Option<Type>` field; only the parser never populated it
# for the pipe-lambda form.
#
# Fix: both functions now optionally consume `: <Type>` (via the existing
# `parse_type()`) after each pipe-lambda parameter name, mirroring `fn`
# parameter parsing. Scope was deliberately limited to the `|...|` pipe-lambda
# syntax; the backslash-lambda form (`\x: body`) already uses a bare trailing
# `:` to end its parameter list and start the body, so adding `: Type` there
# would be ambiguous and was left untouched.
#
# STATUS AT WRITE TIME: fixed only in the seed parser source
# (src/compiler_rust/parser/**). The deployed `bin/simple` is currently the
# unrebuilt Rust seed (see .claude/rules/bootstrap.md "KNOWN BLOCKER"), and
# this task's constraints forbid running a bootstrap rebuild. The `simple-parser`
# crate was confirmed to compile cleanly with the fix
# (`cargo build -p simple-parser`), but the deployed binary does not yet
# contain it, so this spec is expected to stay RED under `bin/simple test`
# until the next seed/bootstrap rebuild picks up the parser change.

describe "typed pipe-lambda parameters":
    it "a single typed param parses and calls correctly":
        val f = |x: i64| x + 1
        expect(f(5)).to_equal(6)

    it "multiple typed params parse and call correctly":
        val g = |x: i64, y: i64| x + y
        expect(g(3, 4)).to_equal(7)

    it "untyped params still parse correctly (no regression)":
        val h = |x| x + 1
        expect(h(5)).to_equal(6)
